{"name":"Hyacinth Violet","hex":"#9b4d93","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hyacinth","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any bulbous plant of the genus Hyacinthus, native to the Mediterranean and South Africa.","A variety of zircon, ranging in color from brown, orange, reddish-brown and yellow; a jacinth."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hyacinth"},{"term":"violet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant or flower of the genus Viola, especially the fragrant Viola odorata;  similar-looking plants and flowers.","A person thought to resemble V. odorata, especially in its beauty and delicacy."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a violet colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/violet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hyacinth","blocks":[{"text":"Hyacinthus is a genus of bulbous herbs and spring-blooming perennials. They are fragrant flowering plants in the family Asparagaceae, subfamily Scilloideae and are commonly called hyacinths. The genus is native predominantly to the Eastern Mediterranean region from the south of Turkey to the Palestine region, although naturalized more widely."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Hyacinth_-_Anglesey_Abbey.jpg/330px-Hyacinth_-_Anglesey_Abbey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyacinth","wikidata":"Q158758"},{"title":"Violet (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Violet is the color of light at the short wavelength end of the visible spectrum. It is one of the seven colors that Isaac Newton labeled when dividing the spectrum of visible light in 1704. Violet light has a wavelength between approximately 380 and 435 nanometers. The color's name is derived from the Viola genus of flowers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Amatista_Laye_2.jpg/330px-Amatista_Laye_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumb